--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party source code
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

No third-party source code is vendored in this repository. The IW4L and
mashup-launcher binaries link the crates declared in Cargo.toml, each under its
own licence, and none of the projects listed under "References consulted
during development". Besides the two fonts above, mashup-launcher embeds the
four egui fonts listed under "Linux AppImage" below, and the AppImage bundles
three separate prebuilt tools next to the binaries.

The fork carries two projects of its own author's in-tree, built as part of
the workspace:

skate/ — the Skate 3 skating engine and its data converter. It reads the
  player's own copy of Skate 3; no Skate 3 code or data is included.

third_party/minecraftoss/ — five crates of MinecraftOSS, a Rust Minecraft
  engine, copied from its commit 4013a68 (see its README). Its few data
  tables are gameplay values measured from Minecraft (food, waxing and
  dispenser rules). No Minecraft code, JARs, textures or sounds are included:
  the game downloads Minecraft's files from Mojang on first run.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Linux AppImage
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Linux AppImage release (packaging/linux/build-appimage.sh) carries the
following on top of the iw4l binary and the fonts above. Licence texts are
installed under usr/share/doc/2010-rust-rewrite-mashup/ inside the AppImage.

egui fonts, embedded in mashup-launcher by eframe's default_fonts feature
  (the epaint_default_fonts crate). Texts in licenses/egui-fonts/.
  Hack: Copyright 2018 Source Foundry Authors, MIT License, with Bitstream
    Vera portions under the Bitstream Vera License.
    https://github.com/source-foundry/Hack
  Ubuntu Light: Ubuntu Font Licence 1.0. https://design.ubuntu.com/font
  Noto Emoji: SIL Open Font License 1.1.
    https://fonts.google.com/noto/specimen/Noto+Emoji
  emoji-icon-font: Copyright 2014 John Slegers, MIT License.
    https://github.com/jslegers/emoji-icon-font

extract-xiso: https://github.com/XboxDev/extract-xiso
  Used to extract Xbox 360 .iso images. A modified BSD licence, which requires
  reproducing its notice with the binary; the full text is installed at
  usr/share/doc/2010-rust-rewrite-mashup/extract-xiso-LICENSE.txt inside the
  AppImage.

skate-convert: a PyInstaller build of the Skate 3 data converter
  (skate/converter/iw4l_skate_convert.py) against
  https://github.com/SK8-ENGINE/skate-3-rust-engine, rev cb79689. The
  PyInstaller bundle embeds a CPython interpreter, NumPy and Pillow, plus the
  skate engine's own tools/ directory and its third-party notices and vendored
  licences (utt, skate3_ui, university and FBX2glTF). Licence texts for all of
  these are installed under
  usr/share/doc/2010-rust-rewrite-mashup/licenses/ inside the AppImage.
  readline, curses and OpenSSL (ssl/_ssl/_hashlib) are excluded from this
  bundle: the converter only reads local files, so they're unused weight
  (and readline is GPL-3.0, which would otherwise need its own accounting).
  What PyInstaller still pulls in by scanning the interpreter's actual link
  dependencies (libb2, bzip2, expat, libffi, xz (liblzma), mpdecimal,
  util-linux's libuuid and zlib) are plain host system libraries,
  not part of any of the above projects; their licence texts, copied from
  this build host's own package database, are installed under
  usr/share/doc/2010-rust-rewrite-mashup/licenses/system-libs/ inside the
  AppImage.

appimagetool: https://github.com/AppImage/appimagetool
  Used only to build the AppImage; it is not itself redistributed.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
References consulted during development
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

These projects were read while working out how the original games behave. They
are named here because the reading happened, not because anything of theirs is
distributed: none of them is vendored, linked, bundled or otherwise shipped.

OpenAssetTools — https://github.com/Laupetin/OpenAssetTools
  Open-source modding tools for older Call of Duty games. Its asset-structure
  headers document the on-disk layouts IW4L reads. Licensed under GPLv3.

oat (iw4x-x64 fork of OpenAssetTools) — https://github.com/iw4x-x64/oat
  The x64 fork, at revision 3a7442dee5ce040cfdb8da23fdb9fb525b5f743e, is the
  tree whose IW4MS headers were read for the 64-bit FastFile layouts.
  Licensed under GPLv3, like its upstream. No file in this repository is
  compiled against, generated from or distributed with those headers.

IW4x — https://github.com/iw4x/iw4x-client
  A custom client for Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 2 (2009), with dedicated
  servers, security fixes and modding support. Read as a cross-reference for
  asset and protocol behaviour, and by the fork for controller aim assist. Licensed under GPLv3.

KisakCOD — https://github.com/SwagSoftware/KisakCOD
  A Call of Duty 4 open-source reimplementation. Read as a cross-reference for
  engine structure, and by the fork for controller aim assist's defaults. IW4L targets IW4, so this is a neighbouring generation of
  the same engine family, not the same codebase.

Ghidra — https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra
  The software reverse engineering framework the original game binaries were
  read with. A development tool: none of it, and none of its output, is
  committed here or distributed with IW4L. Licensed under Apache 2.0.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Game content
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

No Call of Duty or Skate game data, and no Minecraft code or assets, are
contained in this repository or in any release archive. IW4L reads game data from a copy of the game the user already
owns and has installed. Call of Duty, Modern Warfare, Black Ops, related
trademarks and all original game assets belong to their respective owners.
IW4L is not affiliated with, endorsed by or supported by them.
